    {
      "id": "6afdaa42ee27fc70ccfe0fad",
      "name": "Bazuka: The Fraud Framework That Can Do It All",
      "description": "In late October 2026, a new mobile threat called Bazuka was discovered while investigating an NFC card relay application. Unlike typical banking trojans with simple architectures, Bazuka is a comprehensive modular framework designed for fraudulent operations. The framework consists of a dropper/downloader, core component, and 23 specialized modules covering overlays, keylogging, SMS and 2FA interception, remote control, and cryptocurrency address swapping. Notable features include LLM integration within the control panel to help operators manage infections and identify high-value victims, plus ADB bridging capabilities for covert privilege escalation. Distribution occurs through traffers using lures like adult content, with plans for potential Google Play deployment using minimal permissions to bypass review processes. The control panel, partially in Russian, reveals sophisticated victim management tools and AI-assisted targeting capabilities.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-23T11:02:31.948000",
      "created": "2026-11-18T13:38:10.462000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "",
