    {
      "id": "6b0056c70ff05cc28c4f0d54",
      "name": "October 2026 Infostealer Trend Report",
      "description": "During October 2026, multiple infostealer families including Lzveil, StealC, Remus, DarkCloud, and AgentTesla were distributed through various channels. Distribution methods included SEO poisoning, file-hosting services, cloud storage platforms like Mega and Mediafire, and spear-phishing emails. Malware was frequently disguised as software cracks and keygens, with Microsoft Corporation being the most impersonated entity. EXE files constituted 98.4% of execution types, while DLL side-loading accounted for 1.6%. The newly identified Lzveil infostealer was distributed alongside Remus in password-protected archives. Email-based campaigns involved DarkCloud impersonating laboratory equipment companies and AgentTesla impersonating shipping firms. Both collected extensive system information, credentials, and sensitive data using SMTP and FTP exfiltration methods respectively.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-23T10:45:06.497000",
      "created": "2026-11-20T14:18:47.679000",
      "revision": 2,
