    {
      "id": "6ada1e5699d69ce1b1fa9660",
      "name": "Inside SiriusQT: A Seriously Skilled RAT",
      "description": "SiriusQT is a Windows Remote Access Trojan advertised on dark web forums since April 2026, offering capabilities beyond remote administration including HVNC, credential theft, cryptocurrency wallet collection, clipboard monitoring, keylogging, process injection, and persistence mechanisms. Distributed via Malware-as-a-Service by a reseller named GhostKernel, at least 30 users purchased licenses ranging from monthly to lifetime access. The malware was observed targeting victims in the United Kingdom, Germany, France, Spain, Italy, and the Netherlands. SiriusQT employs custom encrypted command-and-control traffic, multiple process injection techniques, and features both native C++ and .NET loader variants. The threat actor Amonus is referenced as the alleged developer, though GhostKernel appears to be the primary distributor through underground forums and Telegram channels.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-21T14:02:03.875000",
      "created": "2026-10-22T14:31:50.608000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "GhostKernel",
