    {
      "id": "6ad91a624dfadf973556516c",
      "name": "PHP2Shell Vulnerabilities: CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619",
      "description": "Two critical remote code execution vulnerabilities in PHP, collectively named PHP2Shell, were disclosed on October 21, 2026. CVE-2026-109618 involves out-of-memory handling corruption that can interrupt PHP while internal request data is partially initialized, leading to invalid data treatment. CVE-2026-109619 exploits stale global state where request-owned internal state survives after memory is freed. Both vulnerabilities can be exploited successfully on ARM64 systems and x86 systems without ASLR. Fixed versions include PHP 8.5.12, 8.4.27, 8.3.36, and 8.2.35. Over 2.6 million internet-facing hosts running PHP were identified as vulnerable, with 42% on end-of-life branches receiving no official patches. Early exploitation attempts were observed from multiple sources, including scanning activity and webshell deployment attempts targeting the upload-progress vulnerability path.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-20T20:00:37.857000",
      "created": "2026-10-21T20:02:42.764000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "",
