    {
      "id": "6afda8f8ad54682e30c1bb10",
      "name": "Behind the Power Outage: Unmasking a New Intrusion Vector in Enterprise Infrastructure Tools",
      "description": "An attacker exploited CyberPower PowerPanel Business (PPB), a UPS management software, to execute malicious code on a Windows domain controller. The threat actor uploaded custom scripts through the web console's file-upload endpoint on TCP port 3052, leveraging PPB's legitimate feature that runs scripts during power events. These scripts executed with SYSTEM privileges through the trusted ppbed.exe service. The attacker downloaded tools using certutil, loaded .NET assemblies into memory, and extracted Active Directory data using ntdsutil and esentutl against ntds.dit. Through iterative script development, the adversary refined their collection process until successful. Finally, they deployed a persistent implant, upnpcont.exe with malicious atl.dll, using DLL side-loading. The operation demonstrated interactive human engagement, utilizing primarily built-in Windows utilities to maintain stealth while compromising critical infrastructure.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-19T10:49:46.566000",
      "created": "2026-11-18T13:32:40.653000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
