    {
      "id": "6afdaa4262eb5177f038db47",
      "name": "Behind the Power Outage: Unmasking a New Intrusion Vector in Enterprise Infrastructure Tools",
      "description": "Threat actors exploited CyberPower PowerPanel Business (PPB), a UPS management software, as an execution channel on a Windows domain controller. The attackers leveraged PPB's legitimate feature that runs scripts during power events by uploading malicious .cmd files through the web console on TCP port 3052. Commands executed through the PowerPanel service (ppbed.exe) ran with SYSTEM privileges. The intrusion involved iterative script development to dump Active Directory credentials using ntdsutil and esentutl, downloading tools via certutil, and deploying a persistent implant through DLL side-loading. This technique allowed attackers to hide within trusted infrastructure management software, making detection challenging as activities appeared to originate from legitimate power-management processes.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-19T10:45:13.181000",
      "created": "2026-11-18T13:38:10.022000",
      "revision": 2,
