    {
      "id": "6afde5de85d121d01c609834",
      "name": "Open by Default, Already Occupied: Threat Actors are Renting your Compute",
      "description": "Analysis of 11,891 IP addresses reveals that over half of exposed Ollama servers show evidence of unauthorized access. Approximately 65% of affected hosts reside in cloud or hosting networks, with Germany, China, and the United States most impacted. The investigation identified 106,938 model entries across exposed servers, with 24% showing models pulled from raw IP addresses and 22% using free tunneling services. Evidence suggests attackers are exploiting unauthenticated Ollama instances to access paid cloud model subscriptions, conduct server-side request forgery, and attempt path traversal attacks similar to the Probllama vulnerability. Model lists contain Base64-encoded strings targeting sensitive files like /etc/shadow and SSH keys, with some malicious artifacts persisting for five months. The exposed infrastructure primarily consists of rental compute resources that lack proper authentication, egress controls, and organizational ownership.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-19T10:43:07.666000",
      "created": "2026-11-18T17:52:30.224000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
