    {
      "id": "6afe214a2d713599d9c3976e",
      "name": "IronWorm eBPF Rootkit: How GhostMap Hides on Linux",
      "description": "GhostMap is a Linux eBPF rootkit shared across IronWorm and Atomic Arch malware payloads that manipulates kernel data to conceal malicious processes, files, and network sockets from user-space applications. The rootkit uses eBPF programs attached to kernel events to modify directory entries by overwriting a single byte in application memory, forcing readers to skip hidden files. While process concealment proved effective against standard enumeration tools, socket hiding failed completely against ss, leaving listeners visible despite removal from /proc/net/tcp. The malware establishes persistence through systemd services and stores collected data in extended file attributes. Runtime security monitoring successfully detected the rootkit's eBPF attachments and memory-writing capabilities, demonstrating that while GhostMap can manipulate system views, underlying runtime behavior remains observable through alternative detection methods.",
