    {
      "id": "6afc35110b4de5ee65835e87",
      "name": "Lotuslite V2: The Evolution of Mustang Panda's .NET Backdoor",
      "description": "This analysis identifies Lotuslite V2 as a .NET successor to LOTUSLITE malware, attributed to Mustang Panda (Earth Preta). The campaign targets Thailand through a public Zoho WorkDrive link delivering an assessment-themed ZIP archive. The infection chain leverages a signed PDFgear executable for DLL side-loading, establishes registry persistence, and initiates TCP connections to command-and-control infrastructure. The evolved variant introduces the PRO1 protocol marker (0x50524F31) and implements application-layer AES-256-GCM encryption. Key modifications include a transition from C++ to managed .NET/C# architecture, enhanced cryptographic protection beyond transport-layer security, and refined command routing through bitwise MSB masking. The operational tradecraft maintains consistency with previous Mustang Panda campaigns, including signed binary abuse, ProgramData staging directories, and registry-based persistence mechanisms targeting government and diplomatic entities in the ASEAN region.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-17T11:15:55.857000",
      "created": "2026-11-17T11:05:53.115000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "MUSTANG PANDA",
