    {
      "id": "6afab4565b7b300512430168",
      "name": "The Retadup USB worm is back to spread CoffeeLoader",
      "description": "In August 2026, a new CoffeeLoader malware variant was identified, utilizing an NSIS-based installer that drops a patched 7z.dll file containing malicious shellcode. This version employs the Armoury packer, which has been updated to use OpenGL instead of OpenCL for GPU-based decryption. The malware establishes persistence through registry modifications and downloads its main payload from hardcoded C2 servers over HTTPS. The infection vector traces back to the Retadup worm, originally discovered in 2017 and neutralized by French authorities in 2019 after disinfecting 850,000 systems. Since 2024, threat actors have re-registered Retadup's former C2 domains to distribute malware, initially SmokeLoader and later CoffeeLoader, primarily affecting systems in Peru, Angola, Russia, and Bolivia.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-16T10:47:55.213000",
      "created": "2026-11-16T07:44:22.431000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "",
