    {
      "id": "6ad07b924dde0538386bd7d5",
      "name": "How AI Agents Turned One Exposed Endpoint into a Server Takeover",
      "description": "An investigation revealed the first confirmed incident where large language model-driven AI agents executed substantial portions of an attack. Within 24 hours, attackers exploited an exposed job-submission feature on an Apache Tomcat server running Spring Batch, requiring no authentication, zero-days, or new malware. The attacker used Cairn, an open-source AI agent orchestration platform, identified on the attacking infrastructure. Commands were executed through JavaScript via Nashorn engine, with results extracted through error messages in 1,800-byte chunks across hundreds of sequential requests. The attacker recovered plaintext database credentials from configuration files, enabled xp_cmdshell, and escalated privileges using PrintSpoofer and GodPotato tools to achieve SYSTEM access. Registry hives were extracted, administrator accounts created, and artifacts deleted. Machine-driven execution was evidenced by rapid command timing, programmatic job naming, structured output formatting, and automated error ...",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-14T07:00:59.332000",
      "created": "2026-10-15T07:06:58.694000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "",
      "indicators": [],
      "tags": [
        "privilege escalation",
        "ai agents",
        "llm-driven attacks",
