    {
      "id": "6af32dd634e9e56326a25b37",
      "name": "Beyond the clipboard: The evolving tradecraft of ClickFix",
      "description": "Threat actors continue to evolve ClickFix tactics, moving beyond simple clipboard-based social engineering to more sophisticated attack chains. Recent campaigns leverage malvertising through typosquatting, SEO poisoning, and compromised websites to deliver fake verification prompts. These attacks now employ elaborate PowerShell scripts with animated progress bars to mimic legitimate security checks. Observed payloads include Node.js-based RATs like DuskDesk, lateral movement techniques targeting domain controllers via WinRM and SSH tunnels, and steganographic delivery chains hiding executables within PNG files. Some campaigns utilize browser-cache-seeded attacks that detect the victim's browser and retrieve staged payloads from cached files. Defense requires user training to recognize social engineering, application control policies, and behavioral monitoring for suspicious command execution patterns and persistence mechanisms.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-13T14:57:56.493000",
      "created": "2026-11-10T14:44:38.122000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
