    {
      "id": "6af5dfd520ceb5c877234020",
      "name": "0% GPU utilization: How SANSAR hides Linux cryptomining",
      "description": "Elastic Security Labs analyzes REF3364, a sophisticated Linux cryptomining intrusion combining custom malware with public miners. The toolkit features SANSAR, a Rust-based orchestrator managing CPU and GPU miners over Tor infrastructure. Attackers exploited an exposed Ray job agent to gain root access across three Linux GPU hosts. The operation deployed a kernel rootkit concealing processes and files, an NVIDIA Management Library wrapper falsifying GPU telemetry to show 0% utilization, and a backdoor named netns retrieving C2 addresses from Ethereum smart contracts. SANSAR supervises XMRig and SRBMiner-MULTI miners while maintaining command handlers for configuration management and interactive shells. The threat actor employed multiple stealth mechanisms including ftrace-based rootkit hooks, modified GPU monitoring libraries, and Tor-based communications with bridge support for enhanced anonymity.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-12T16:42:49.007000",
      "created": "2026-11-12T15:48:37.397000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "REF3364",
