    {
      "id": "6af4e812bfab56c87bffd06e",
      "name": "The global socket: how threat actors are weaponizing GSocket for stealthy C2",
      "description": "Multiple threat actors have been deploying GSocket, an open source remote access tool, on compromised Linux servers to maintain persistent access even after initial vulnerabilities are patched. The activity increased 356.2% from March through August 2026, primarily targeting web applications and hosting environments, with education and research sectors representing 30.7% of affected organizations. GSocket relays traffic through a shared public network, eliminating traditional C2 infrastructure that defenders could block. Threat actors exploit internet-facing applications, deploy GSocket with renamed processes in memory-backed directories, establish persistence through cron and systemd, and in some cases escalate privileges using CVE-2026-43284. The tool's outbound-only connections and shared relay infrastructure make it particularly difficult to detect and remediate through conventional methods.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-12T10:02:48.306000",
      "created": "2026-11-11T22:11:30.153000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "",
