    {
      "id": "6af46905053d389268987e1d",
      "name": "Citrus Ransomware: tactics, techniques, procedures, and links to Toy Ghouls",
      "description": "Citrus Ransomware represents a hybrid threat combining traditional infrastructure compromise techniques with modern evasion methods and AI-powered automation. The group gains initial access through compromised contractor accounts, using stolen credentials for VPN or RDP connections. Attackers employ VLESS tunnels disguised as HTTPS traffic and leverage large language models to generate commands and scripts. They exploit vulnerabilities like CVE-2026-54121 for privilege escalation, utilize tools including mimikatz and Snaffler for credential theft, and deploy the Sn00py ransomware via Group Policy Objects. The operation involves disabling security solutions, clearing event logs, and harvesting Telegram session data before encryption. The malware uses RSA-2048 and ChaCha20 algorithms, appending .citrus extensions to encrypted files. Links to Toy Ghouls suggest possible connections between Russian-speaking groups targeting Russian organizations exclusively.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-11T16:07:46.184000",
      "created": "2026-11-11T13:09:25.800000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "Citrus Ransomware",
