    {
      "id": "6accdd88406e67c287aa23a5",
      "name": "From Endpoint to Funhouse: Changes in Ransomware Families and Kimsuky-Related Distribution Cases",
      "description": "A North Korean threat actor distributed Funhouse ransomware through malicious Korean-language documents disguised as resumes. Funhouse is a variant of the previously observed Endpoint ransomware. The attack chain involves multi-stage scripts including Batch, VBS, and PowerShell, leading to Quasar RAT installation before deploying the final ransomware payload. The campaign shares significant similarities with earlier Kimsuky Endpoint ransomware operations from early 2026, including identical mutex values, similar batch file structures, and comparable Quasar RAT configurations. The ransomware maintains key characteristics of the Endpoint family while updating encryption extensions, contact information, and C2 infrastructure. A notable shift involves using HWP documents instead of LNK files as initial infection vectors.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-11T13:06:29.554000",
      "created": "2026-10-12T13:15:52.686000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "Kimsuky",
