    {
      "id": "6af36e03fef8cc1180e8a7da",
      "name": "A New ESXi Implant Linked to Settra Ransomware",
      "description": "GhostFrame is a previously undocumented post-exploitation framework enabling extensive control over compromised VMware ESXi environments. After root-level access is obtained, it provides a unified interface for accessing virtual machines, harvesting credentials, executing commands, and pivoting into victim networks. The framework operates from the virtualization layer, allowing attackers to control multiple VMs from a single compromised ESXi host. TAG-218, whose operations overlap with Settra ransomware, has been observed deploying GhostFrame following compromise of VPN appliances across the US and Europe. The implant establishes persistence, disables security controls, uses distinctive C2 communications across multiple ports, and integrates credential harvesting tools including VMkatz. This demonstrates how compromised hypervisors serve as centralized platforms for post-exploitation activities across virtualized environments.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-11T09:30:54.059000",
      "created": "2026-11-10T19:18:27.757000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "TAG-218",
      "indicators": [
