    {
      "id": "6ac84a166254b974f23968a2",
      "name": "MATCHBOIL: New tricks, same old evil intentions",
      "description": "ESET researchers documented the evolution of MATCHBOIL, a custom C# downloader used by Russia-aligned APT group UAC-0099. The malware downloads payloads from command-and-control servers, installs them, and establishes persistence on victim machines. Development traces back to April 2024, with continuous improvements through April 2026. Early versions used Unicode symbol obfuscation and custom string encryption, while later iterations employed the Eziriz .NET Reactor obfuscator. UAC-0099 progressively added sandbox detection methods, modified persistence mechanisms from registry keys to scheduled tasks, and introduced deceptive graphical interfaces. All observed victims were Ukrainian organizations across transportation, manufacturing, and energy sectors. The group typically distributes MATCHBOIL through spearphishing emails containing malicious links that download VBScript files. MATCHBOIL often delivers MATCHWOK backdoor as its final payload.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-08T01:03:47.635000",
      "created": "2026-10-09T01:57:42.729000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "UAC-0099",
      "indicators": [
