    {
      "id": "6aec3ce29e33d6b94efd4c0f",
      "name": "Binance heist: what we know, and what we see in TraderTraitor's toolkit",
      "description": "In October 2026, attackers stole approximately $6.34 billion from Binance in one of the largest cryptocurrency thefts on record. The FBI attributed the attack to North Korea's TraderTraitor operation. The multi-stage compromise involved malicious software delivered through a fake job interview, where engineers were tricked into downloading trojanized developer tools including a weaponized Terraform provider. ReversingLabs analysis uncovered malicious components in their telemetry 77 days before public disclosure and identified backdoor variants with earlier submission histories. The attack chain progressed from initial developer workstation compromise through lateral movement to ultimately reach Binance's wallet signing infrastructure. The trojanized tools included FLATROOF and ROOFDECK backdoors hidden within legitimate-looking developer software, demonstrating sophisticated supply chain compromise techniques targeting DevOps environments.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-05T09:56:45.927000",
      "created": "2026-11-05T08:22:58.582000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "TraderTraitor",
