      "id": "6aeb40b674ccf1e5c2eb0bd3",
      "name": "Not Actually Suspended: How a Bank-Phishing Network Fakes Its Own Takedown",
      "description": "A sophisticated phishing campaign targets 189 US financial institutions through 248 live look-alike domains using a distinctive naming pattern combining urgency keywords with bank names. The operation employs an innovative cloaking mechanism that displays fake \"account suspended\" pages to returning visitors while serving actual phishing content to first-time visitors, effectively evading takedown efforts. The infrastructure focuses on business banking and treasury portals, which provide access to larger balances and transfer capabilities. All domains operate behind Cloudflare, with hosting traced to shared servers at two providers. Network analysis reveals connections to a centralized backend managing additional phishing operations targeting UK, Netherlands, Australian, and New Zealand institutions. The campaign is administered through a jump server accessed via Remote Desktop from a UK residential broadband connection, representing the strongest lead to the operator's identity.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-04T19:37:53.265000",
      "created": "2026-11-04T14:27:02.788000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "",
