    {
      "id": "6ae8e4db45e485be0d9c99d9",
      "name": "The Rise of LLMJacking: How Threat Actors Monetize Compromised Cloud and Model Resources",
      "description": "Threat actors are hijacking enterprise access to Large Language Models through a tactic called LLMJacking, impacting organizations across North America, South America, and Asia Pacific in financial services, retail, technology, and transportation sectors. Unlike cryptojacking's gradual consumption, LLMJacking generates massive spikes in fraudulent cloud charges, with incidents exceeding $100,000 USD daily and reaching $1 million USD cumulatively. Actors establish initial access through exposed cloud identity tokens, IAM credentials, Service Account keys, and stolen API keys. They escalate privileges, conduct reconnaissance, and monetize compromised resources through LLM Mixers—illicit routing gateways that proxy and resell unauthorized LLM access. The ecosystem shows sophisticated specialization with distinct operational clusters coordinating compromises and downstream monetization infrastructure.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-03T09:58:36.174000",
      "created": "2026-11-02T19:30:35.625000",
      "revision": 2,
