    {
      "id": "6ae58670747ffa49b026a7f5",
      "name": "North Korea's Crypto Heists Start on the Open Internet",
      "description": "North Korean operators execute cryptocurrency thefts by initially compromising developers through social engineering on the open internet before moving operations on-chain. The Bybit theft in February 2025 began when operators tricked a Safe{Wallet} engineer into running a malicious stock-trading project via getstockprice[.]com, later planting code in Bybit's wallet interface. Binance experienced similar compromise through a DevOps engineer's laptop. These operations rely on registering domains before targeting victims, with bybit-assessment[.]com registered hours before the Bybit theft. An investigation revealed three US-registered front companies (BlockNovas, Angeloper Agency, and SoftGlide) used to deliver malware through fake job interviews. Preemptive defense mechanisms identified 206 Binance-named domains with maximum risk scores, with 64 showing live activity, enabling defenders to block malicious infrastructure before deployment.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-02T23:02:46.219000",
      "created": "2026-10-31T06:10:56.622000",
      "revision": 2,
