    {
      "id": "6ae4c7d56e2e1e8caa32e069",
      "name": "From help desk impersonation to AD CS abuse: Investigating ESC1 and ESC8 intrusion paths",
      "description": "Two human-operated intrusions leveraged Microsoft Teams messages and calls to impersonate IT personnel, socially engineering users into granting remote access through Quick Assist. Threat actors staged portable Python, Bun, or Java runtimes, performed network discovery, and assessed Active Directory Certificate Services for authentication paths. The incidents diverged at the certificate layer: one exploited an ESC1-vulnerable certificate template to obtain authentication certificates for privileged accounts and performed DCSync operations, while the other used PetitPotam to coerce domain-controller authentication and relayed it to AD CS Web Enrollment through ESC8, obtaining a domain-controller certificate for Kerberos authentication. Automated attack disruption limited progression in the first case except for an excluded privileged identity that enabled data exfiltration, while customer-led device isolation stopped progression in the second case following Defender alerts.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-11-02T22:51:35.227000",
      "created": "2026-10-30T16:37:41.836000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "",
