    {
      "id": "6ae45373ec45a11f4f945be7",
      "name": "Inside Coruna and DarkSword Chains: From Web Lures to iOS Exploitation",
      "description": "Seven months after initial disclosure, the Coruna iOS exploit framework continues to evolve, now deploying two distinct chains based on device operating system version. Targets running iOS 17.2.1 or earlier receive the legacy Coruna framework, while later builds receive DarkSword-derived chains. Analysis identified 608 domains delivering Coruna-family code, with 93 sites mapping to 21 distinct tracking collectors. Distribution spans dating portals, AI-companion platforms, cryptocurrency promotions, media streaming services, and compromised legitimate sites. The attack requires no user interaction beyond loading a webpage, operating through hidden iframes that deploy browser exploits leading to native code execution. Recovered modules include kernel memory manipulation, code-signing enforcement changes, process memory access, encrypted command-and-control communication, and cleanup routines. One delivery vector involves compromised npm packages, enabling legitimate websites to serve exploits through their o...",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-10-30T09:58:17.408000",
      "created": "2026-10-30T08:21:07.606000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "",
