    {
      "id": "6ae3cb79a3fb666626c21e74",
      "name": "How Attackers Redirect Crypto Payments",
      "description": "Cryptocurrency transactions face unique threats from specialized malware known as Clipbankers, designed to redirect payments by replacing copied wallet addresses with attacker-controlled ones. These attacks exploit user trust in clipboard functions, the difficulty of memorizing long cryptographic strings, and the irreversible nature of blockchain transactions. Two prominent families, AnimateClipper and Efimer, demonstrate sophisticated capabilities including pattern matching and digit-matching techniques to minimize detection. AnimateClipper emerged in January 2026 and monitors clipboards for cryptocurrency patterns across multiple networks. Efimer employs advanced evasion techniques including Pyarmor protection, XOR encryption, and TOR-based command and control communications, while matching leading or trailing digits of addresses. Both families are distributed through social engineering methods including ClickFix campaigns, phishing emails, and fake software installations. The malware families became lar...",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-10-30T09:37:56.341000",
      "created": "2026-10-29T22:40:57.761000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
