    {
      "id": "6ae38bc6be61d23c0f7a2165",
      "name": "Inside Coruna and DarkSword Chains: From Web Lures to iOS Exploitation",
      "description": "Seven months after initial disclosure, the Coruna iOS exploit framework continues evolving with two distinct exploit chains conditioned on device operating system versions. Targets running iOS 17.2.1 or earlier receive the legacy Coruna framework, while later builds receive DarkSword-derived execution chains. Through October 2026, 608 domains were identified delivering Coruna-family code across dating portals, AI-companion platforms, cryptocurrency promotions, and compromised legitimate sites. The attack operates entirely through web pages via hidden iframes, requiring no application installation or user consent. Recovered native modules demonstrate sophisticated capabilities including kernel memory manipulation, Apple Mobile File Integrity state changes, process memory reads, encrypted command-and-control communication, and artifact deletion. A supply-chain compromise through the npm package art-template enables delivery through legitimate application infrastructure, bypassing traditional domain-based def...",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-10-30T08:22:29.002000",
      "created": "2026-10-29T18:09:10.746000",
      "revision": 3,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "",
