    {
      "id": "6ae34b9375e99ba3a966f469",
      "name": "MadApe: The Code That Opens the Door",
      "description": "A previously undocumented Microsoft Device Code phishing kit named MadApe has been uncovered, operating through a multi-stage campaign that combines business-themed PDF lures, configurable redirect chains, and fake document-sharing pages. The kit abuses Microsoft's legitimate Device Code authentication flow to gain access to victim accounts without collecting credentials on phishing pages. The campaign demonstrates sophisticated infrastructure comprising 155 root domains registered between June and August 2026, primarily through Dynadot. Multiple phishing templates impersonate SharePoint, OneDrive, Teams, Adobe Acrobat, and DocuSign while sharing identical backend functionality. The infrastructure includes custom human-verification challenges, decoy websites using shared templates, and operator panels exposed on the same hosts serving phishing pages. The activity shows broad localization across at least nine languages and targets business-related communications.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-10-29T16:09:10.003000",
      "created": "2026-10-29T13:35:15.641000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "",
