    {
      "id": "6ae1affbaef99b0bc3777283",
      "name": "September 2026 Infostealer Trend Report",
      "description": "During September 2026, infostealers were primarily distributed through SEO poisoning, disguised as illegal software such as cracks and keygens. Major distribution platforms included Mediafire, AWS S3, Mega, and Cloudflare Storage. EXE files comprised 90.8% of samples, while DLL side-loading accounted for 9.2%, utilizing legitimate files like python37.dll and POWRPROF.dll. A new infostealer named Lzveil, built with Rust, downloads configuration data from C2 servers and uses modified Base64 decryption, XOR encryption, and LZNT1 compression. Email campaigns distributed DarkCloud and AgentTesla infostealers, impersonating construction and logistics companies respectively. Both steal credentials, browser data, cryptocurrency wallets, and system information. The stolen data is often traded on dark web markets or used for secondary attacks.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-10-28T10:32:17.432000",
      "created": "2026-10-28T08:19:07.897000",
      "revision": 2,
