    {
      "id": "6ae0cc28c4875a80ccd6b804",
      "name": "Camera Management Software Compromise Leads to Malware Deployment",
      "description": "Attackers compromised camera management software (CMS.exe) to gain initial access to multiple organizational environments. Following compromise, adversaries deployed rs.win32.exe into user AppData directories and leveraged DLL sideloading by placing the legitimate Windows binary WpcMon.exe alongside malicious DLLs (IPHLPAPI.DLL and samcli.dll) in non-standard locations. These modified DLLs, based on zlib compression libraries, incorporated beaconing functionality to communicate with a command-and-control server at 144.172.116.37. Persistence was established through Run Key registry modifications. The camera management software, potentially linked to 3R Global and kvision.ai, lacked adequate security controls and logging capabilities. Organizations should treat camera systems and their management platforms as critical components of the enterprise attack surface, implementing proper access controls, monitoring unusual process chains, and restricting executable execution in user-writable paths.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-10-27T16:44:43.100000",
      "created": "2026-10-27T16:07:04.299000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "",
