    {
      "id": "6ae0b192bfaf2df2545701f0",
      "name": "Tricks from the Past deployed via signed ChatGPT and Claude binaries",
      "description": "Check Point Research discovered a campaign tracked as Yoba that exploits the popularity of AI tools like ChatGPT and Claude. Attackers created fake GitHub projects mimicking desktop applications such as GPT-5.6 Sol, GPT-6 Astra, and Claude Opus. These projects host malicious installers alongside legitimate, signed executables from OpenAI and Anthropic, employing DLL sideloading techniques to bypass security protections. The campaign demonstrates strong operational security by avoiding dedicated command-and-control infrastructure, instead relying on Telegram, Tor, Catbox, and DNS over HTTPS. The malware chain includes YobaDropper and YobaLoader components that ultimately deliver unwanted software, including XMRig cryptocurrency miners, to infected systems. The threat actor exhibits indicators suggesting Russian origins and operates primarily in the UTC-4 timezone, excluding CIS countries from infection.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-10-27T16:30:12.295000",
      "created": "2026-10-27T14:13:38.925000",
      "revision": 2,
