    {
      "id": "6ad8d885956829eafc1fa8d1",
      "name": "The Science of Exploitability: Decoding BlueHammer",
      "description": "BlueHammer (CVE-2026-33825) is an eight-stage local privilege-escalation chain targeting Windows Defender Antimalware Platform before version 4.18.26030.3011. It requires initial code execution as a standard user and chains CloudFilter callbacks, batch oplocks, object-manager symlinks, Defender's update RPC, offline SAM parsing, and SAMR operations to achieve NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM access. The exploit leverages legitimate Windows mechanisms rather than memory corruption, freezing MsMpEng.exe through CloudFilter manipulation while staging malicious update files. In laboratory testing, it successfully escalated privileges, recovered domain-admin credentials from LSASS, and enabled lateral movement via pass-the-hash to compromise domain controllers. Runtime factors including pending signature updates, WinDefend state, filter drivers, VSS, and CloudFilter reachability determine actual exploitability beyond version-based detection.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-10-22T09:21:39.467000",
      "created": "2026-10-21T15:21:41.466000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
