    {
      "id": "6ad7d22ab42c70ab00513045",
      "name": "Inside Wazza: A Newly Observed Device Code Phishing Kit Targeting Microsoft 365 Accounts",
      "description": "A newly observed multi-stage phishing kit named Wazza has been actively targeting Microsoft 365 accounts since early July 2026. The campaign delivers phishing emails from compromised accounts containing PDF lures with business themes like invoices and contracts. These PDFs redirect victims through a complex infrastructure leveraging Google Sites, CloudFlare Workers, and various domains. Wazza features dynamic capabilities including 19 different phishing themes impersonating services like Adobe, DocuSign, and OneDrive, along with six corporate decoy websites. The kit employs sophisticated browser interrogation, anti-automation checks, and client-side protection mechanisms. The final stage abuses Microsoft OAuth device authorization, displaying a verification code and instructing victims to complete authentication on a separate page. Throughout the process, a backend session continuously monitors the authentication attempt, ultimately granting attackers access to victim accounts and cloud resources.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-10-21T06:49:23.165000",
      "created": "2026-10-20T20:42:18.268000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
