    {
      "id": "6ad188cfaac94d891e960fa6",
      "name": "Variola: From ClickFix to a Modular Implant",
      "description": "Blackpoint's Adversary Pursuit Group traced a ClickFix intrusion to a previously unclassified modular Windows implant designated Variola. The malware establishes persistent host identity through encrypted storage, maintains configuration and controller information across executions, and employs authenticated command-and-control with controller rotation capabilities. Variola's modular architecture enables operators to dynamically load additional components for credential theft, browser manipulation, and session hijacking. The framework demonstrates capabilities typical of banking trojans, including Chromium browser process injection, live cookie database access, and VNC-style remote access. Multiple related builds discovered in the same GitHub repository suggest active development and experimentation. The implant uses XSalsa20 encryption, secp256k1 key exchange, and Ed25519 signatures for secure communications. Its ability to preserve context, migrate between processes, and expand capabilities on demand tra...",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-10-16T07:20:02.651000",
      "created": "2026-10-16T02:15:43.411000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "",
