    {
      "id": "6ac778436ef05de8abf44e67",
      "name": "The state of AI-analysis evasion in malware",
      "description": "Malware authors are developing techniques to obstruct automated AI analysis by embedding natural-language instructions within their code. This 'AI-analysis evasion' targets the pipeline that extracts text from samples and submits it to language models for triage or classification. Analysis of 84 samples across four malware families (FRUITSHELL, PLOTSAFE, HOLLOWCLAD, and MANTLEMAZE) from January 2025 through July 2026 reveals progression from simple comments claiming benign functionality to template spraying across multiple LLM formats and intimidation tactics invoking copyright, government contracts, and security certifications. The cheapest technique—direct instructions to ignore samples—proved most effective, steering outcomes favorably for attackers in approximately 35% of test runs. However, these techniques must remain in plaintext, making them consistently detectable. The fundamental defense requires treating extracted text as evidence rather than instruction, ensuring analysis pipelines maintain exp...",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-10-08T14:16:58.231000",
      "created": "2026-10-08T11:02:27.984000",
      "revision": 2,
      "tlp": "white",
      "public": 1,
      "adversary": "",
