    {
      "id": "6ab59fabb977cbc4847b4dd1",
      "name": "Trust and the enticing consultancy offer",
      "description": "A social engineering campaign targeting security professionals uses fake consultancy offers and job opportunities to exploit trust. Attackers create sparse social media profiles claiming to be consultants, offering $300 for simple consultations on topics like digital transformation. The initial phone call serves as screening to identify targets with valuable access or knowledge. If successful, targets are asked to provide increasingly sensitive information through written reports, requiring them to abuse trusted access, probe internal systems, or leverage professional relationships. Similar variants include fake recruiters offering prestigious positions while requiring installation of trojanised software. These attacks exploit professional flattery and overconfidence, manipulating security practitioners into compromising their most valuable asset: professional integrity and trusted relationships within the industry.",
      "author_name": "AlienVault",
      "modified": "2026-09-25T14:14:49.175000",
      "created": "2026-09-24T22:09:47.912000",
      "revision": 2,
